    feat(fwnode): expose decoded hardware CPU identity
    
    Add a neutral `FwNode::hardware_cpu_id()` capability for architecture-defined
    hardware CPU identity. The value is not a logical CPU index, firmware-node
    identity or ACPI Processor UID: zero is valid, absence and malformed
    declarations remain distinct, and backends without the capability return
    `Unsupported` by default. The contract does not build a `RawCpuId` or establish
    controller reachability.
    
    Implement the device-tree backend through the public `rs_fdtree::CpuNode`
    view. It classifies `device_type = "cpu"` nodes and decodes their identity from
    `reg` using the parent `#address-cells`, with one-cell compatibility when that
    property is absent. Only one- and two-cell identities fit the result; truncated,
    oversized or extra-cell payloads are rejected instead of being silently
    truncated. The view performs no logical-CPU lookup, MMIO translation or
    controller admission.
    
    The transitional `of` CPU helpers now delegate to the same view:
    `is_cpu_node` uses `CpuNode::from_node`, and `cpu_node_reg` uses
    `CpuNode::hardware_id`. This removes the second independent CPU `reg` decoder,
    so CPU enumeration and firmware hardware-identity queries share one parsing
    rule. `of` keeps only its enabled-status policy and maps malformed identities to
    `None` for its legacy `Option` API.
    
    Add host fixtures for zero, one- and two-cell identities, non-CPU nodes,
    missing identity declarations, missing address-cell defaults and malformed
    declarations. Document the contract, DT boundary and OF delegation.

